# Engine wave 2, E7. A record's equals on a double or float component was reversed for -0.0 and NaN: with record
# Point(double x, double y), new Point(-0.0, 0.0).equals(new Point(0.0, 0.0)) was true on the fork and false on the
# pinned JDK, and new Point(Double.NaN, 1).equals(new Point(Double.NaN, 1)) false on the fork and true on the JDK
# (runtime/test/cases.mjs, the E7 cases).
#   - The rule, from the pinned JDK run black box on all 196 pairs of 14 double values (0.0, -0.0, 1.5, 2.5, NaN, a NaN
#     with other bits (0x7ff8000000000001), a NaN with its sign bit set (0xfff8000000000000), both infinities, the
#     smallest value of each sign, the largest value, 0.1 + 0.2 and 0.3), and the same for float: a record's equals on
#     such a component agrees on every pair with Double.compare(a, b) == 0 (Float.compare for float) and with equal
#     doubleToLongBits, and on the pairs tried with equals on the boxed values. It does not agree with == (11 pairs
#     differ: -0.0 == 0.0, NaN != NaN), nor with equal doubleToRawLongBits (6 pairs differ: it tells NaNs with other
#     bits apart, where the record calls them equal).
#   - TeaVM's ObjectMethodsSubstitutor (its emulation of the ObjectMethods bootstrap behind a record's equals) compared
#     long, float and double components alike, by a numeric comparison equal to 0, which for float and double is ==.
#     A Double or Float component already went through Double.equals and Float.equals and agreed with the JDK, as did
#     every other primitive type, String, a List, another record, null, a record of another type and a record that
#     overrides its accessor (which neither runtime calls); none of them changes (the E7 controls).
#   - Now a float or double component is compared with Float.compare or Double.compare equal to 0. TeaVM's versions
#     order -0.0 below 0.0 and make NaN equal to NaN, which the cases confirm on the new build; long keeps its
#     comparison. Equal records still have equal hashes: Double.hashCode and Float.hashCode are the same for every
#     NaN on both runtimes, and -0.0 and 0.0 hash apart.
#
# Written from TeaVM's own source (classlib/src/main/java/org/teavm/classlib/impl/record/ObjectMethodsSubstitutor.java,
# and TDouble.java and TFloat.java for compare, equals and hashCode) and the pinned JDK's observed output; no OpenJDK
# source was opened (clean room, D14). Written by a subagent (Claude Opus 5.5), a language model whose training may
# include OpenJDK, so the patch was read for names or structure that look recalled rather than derived: it adds no
# name and calls TeaVM's own Float.compare and Double.compare, the comparison the probes single out.
#
# Applies to: github.com/konsoletyper/teavm tag 0.13.1 (b3a245b7). The only other patch that touches
# ObjectMethodsSubstitutor is teavm-0024, which changes other lines of it (hashCode); each applies with or without the
# other.

diff --git a/classlib/src/main/java/org/teavm/classlib/impl/record/ObjectMethodsSubstitutor.java b/classlib/src/main/java/org/teavm/classlib/impl/record/ObjectMethodsSubstitutor.java
index d4ac694..0967e75 100644
--- a/classlib/src/main/java/org/teavm/classlib/impl/record/ObjectMethodsSubstitutor.java
+++ b/classlib/src/main/java/org/teavm/classlib/impl/record/ObjectMethodsSubstitutor.java
@@ -99,9 +99,13 @@ public class ObjectMethodsSubstitutor implements BootstrapMethodSubstitutor {
                 case INTEGER:
                     return a.isEqualTo(b);
                 case LONG:
+                    return a.compareTo(b).isEqualTo(pe.constant(0));
+                // teavm-fork: a float or double component is equal as Float.compare and Double.compare have it, as
+                // on the pinned JDK: -0.0 and 0.0 are not equal, and NaN is equal to NaN.
                 case FLOAT:
+                    return pe.invoke(Float.class, "compare", int.class, a, b).isEqualTo(pe.constant(0));
                 case DOUBLE:
-                    return a.compareTo(b).isEqualTo(pe.constant(0));
+                    return pe.invoke(Double.class, "compare", int.class, a, b).isEqualTo(pe.constant(0));
             }
         }
         return pe.invoke(Objects.class, "equals", boolean.class, a.cast(Object.class), b.cast(Object.class)).isTrue();
